Acceptance criteria (IMMUTABLE ticket intent):
- (none)
Description: Original Requirements (discovery)  AS a Content Author, I WANT to preview draft entries in CMS, SO THAT I can see how content will be shown on the website
ability to preview draft entries in CMS
Notes& Assumptions  Limitation: Might not be possible to preview protected pages and content.
Original Total Estimate:  8  Original FE Estimate:  8  Original DAM Estimate:  0  Original BE Estimate:  0

Proposed verification criteria:
1. Given the Contentstack SDK client is mocked to return a draft entry when a live_preview_token is supplied, the rendered page displays the draft entry's title and body text instead of the published entry's title and body text
2. Given a request URL that includes live_preview=1 and live_preview_token=<token> and content_type_uid=<uid> query parameters, the Contentstack SDK query issued by getEntry or getSingleEntry or findSingletonEntry includes those preview parameters in its request to the Contentstack API
3. Given a request URL that does NOT include live_preview query parameters, the rendered page displays the same published content it displayed before this change, with no difference in layout, data, or behavior
4. Given the Contentstack Stack initialization options object, it contains a live_preview configuration property that enables the SDK's live preview capability
5. Given the Contentstack SDK client is mocked to return a draft entry, and the application receives preview parameters via the request URL, the page re-renders with the draft content without requiring a full page reload
6. Given a protected or authenticated page route, when preview parameters are present in the request URL, the page renders its current published content unchanged (acknowledged limitation — preview is not supported on protected pages)

Apply these rules EXACTLY (the producer is held to the same text — do not invent stricter or looser criteria):
A verification criterion states WHAT AN END USER OR TESTER OBSERVES on the user-facing surface THE TICKET IS ABOUT — the rendered output for an output ticket, the displayed screen for a UI ticket, the response a CLIENT receives for an API ticket. It is a BLACK-BOX check on that surface. It NEVER describes HOW the value is produced.
A verification criterion is FORBIDDEN if it:
- prescribes HOW to implement — any algorithm, mechanism, approach, or the addition/reading of any new field, flag or service;
- references an INTERNAL structure that merely FEEDS the ticket's surface — an intermediate payload, a data-transfer object, or a specific response field used to BUILD the output. Verify the surface the ticket names, NOT the data structure behind it;
- makes a CROSS-COMPARISON that presumes a mechanism — never assert one value "must equal" / "matches" / "is the same as" another; that presumes a shared derivation. Assert the required value is present and correct ON ITS OWN.
Every verification criterion must be observable, testable, and tied to the ticket's stated symptom/intent.

FLAG any verification criterion that violates ANY rule above, OR that fails to cover the intent of an acceptance criterion.
Output ONLY a JSON array of short flag strings, e.g. ["VC 2 prescribes <an approach> — restate as observable outcome"]. Output [] if every VC is clean. No prose, no markdown.

